---
/**
 * THE POST-INSTALL REGISTRATION ASK.
 *
 * WHY IT EXISTS HERE AND NOT IN FRONT OF THE INSTALL. Antonio's first proposal
 * was to require registration IN ORDER to install as a PWA. It was rejected on
 * two grounds, and both are written down because the idea is a natural one and
 * will come back:
 *
 *   1. IT IS NOT TECHNICALLY POSSIBLE. Installing is a BROWSER function, not a
 *      site function: Chrome's own menu offers it, and on iOS it is Safari's
 *      Share then Add to Home Screen. A website cannot stand in front of
 *      either. Worse, the offline precache already runs on a first visit with
 *      no account at all (proven with the origin process killed, in
 *      scripts/test-offline-langs.mjs), so gating OUR button would have hidden
 *      the recommendation without gating a single byte of the benefit.
 *   2. IT WAS THE WRONG MOMENT. The install recommendation lands on /saved,
 *      thirty seconds after a rescue worked, on a site whose homepage promises
 *      "Free, no account". A form on that screen turns a rescue into a
 *      transaction at the exact moment trust is highest, which is the most
 *      expensive possible moment to spend it.
 *
 * So the order is: install with zero friction, then ask an already-committed
 * user, INSIDE the installed app, where the ask is attractive rather than a
 * toll. Somebody reading this from a home screen icon has already decided.
 *
 * IT IS THE PAYOFF, NOT A REQUEST. The heading states what they have just
 * gained ("The rescue is on your phone"), and the body names two things they
 * get, both of which exist and are already built: registration syncs the
 * checklist across devices, and it emails the printable Emergency Card.
 *
 * WHEN IT SHOWS, all four conditions, in this order:
 *   - the page is being viewed as an INSTALLED APP (display-mode: standalone,
 *     or navigator.standalone on iOS). In a normal tab this renders nothing
 *     visible, ever.
 *   - the visitor is NOT already registered. SMG_UNLOCKED and SMG_EMAIL are the
 *     site's existing registration keys, written by EmailCapture and by the
 *     checklist gate and read by both, so this needs no new state to know.
 *   - it has not been dismissed. Its own key, once ever, across both surfaces.
 *   - the page is allowed to carry it (below).
 *
 * WHERE: the language home page and /checklist. NOT /saved, which is where the
 * INSTALL recommendation lives: two asks on one screen is nagging, and the one
 * that belongs on /saved is the one that does not want anything. Not
 * /emergency and nothing under /protocol, via the same insideEmergencyMode()
 * guard, because nothing is ever sold inside Emergency Mode.
 *
 * IT CAN NEVER COLLIDE WITH InstallNudge, and that is structural rather than
 * careful: the nudge returns early WHEN standalone, this returns early UNLESS
 * standalone. On /checklist, where both are rendered into the page, exactly one
 * of them can ever be visible. The gate test asserts it.
 *
 * NO SECOND FORM. It renders EmailCapture, the site's one registration form and
 * its one endpoint, with this component's own heading and body. EmailCapture
 * already handles the whole path: validation, the /api/subscribe call, writing
 * the registration keys, the "already registered" one-tap variant, and the live
 * region for errors. A second form would have been a second thing to keep
 * working, and the reason that form exists in its current shape is that it was
 * silently broken for a while (see its own header).
 *
 * SHIPS HIDDEN AND IS REVEALED BY SCRIPT, the pattern TranslationNotice
 * established: an installed-app check cannot run at build time, so rendering it
 * visible and hiding it in a browser tab would move the page after paint on
 * every normal visit. Hidden first, revealed only for the small set of readers
 * it is for.
 */
import EmailCapture from './EmailCapture.astro';
import { t } from '../i18n/ui';
import { DEFAULT_LANG, insideEmergencyMode } from '../i18n/registry';

interface Props {
  lang?: string;
  /** English canonical path of the host page. */
  path: string;
}
const { lang = DEFAULT_LANG, path } = Astro.props;

const S = t(lang).postInstall;
const B = t(lang).installBanner;

// The guard lives in the component. A call site cannot opt into Emergency Mode,
// and cannot put this on /saved, where the install recommendation already is.
const show = !insideEmergencyMode(path) && path !== '/saved';
---
{show && (
  <div class="pi" id="piWrap" hidden>
    <EmailCapture source="post_install" lang={lang} title={S.head} blurb={S.body} />
    <button type="button" class="pi-close" id="piClose" aria-label={B.dismiss}>&times;</button>
  </div>
)}

{show && (
  <script is:inline>
    (function () {
      var wrap = document.getElementById('piWrap');
      if (!wrap) return;

      // 1. INSTALLED APP ONLY. This is the whole premise: the reader got here
      //    from an icon on their home screen, so they have already committed
      //    and the ask is a payoff rather than a toll. In a browser tab this
      //    element stays hidden forever and costs one function call.
      var standalone = (window.matchMedia &&
                        window.matchMedia('(display-mode: standalone)').matches) ||
                       window.navigator.standalone === true;
      if (!standalone) return;

      // 2. NOT ALREADY REGISTERED. The site's existing keys, written by
      //    EmailCapture and by the checklist gate. Either one saying they are
      //    in is enough to stay quiet: asking a registered reader to register
      //    is the single most obvious way to look like nobody is home.
      //    This is also what makes a successful submit permanent, with no extra
      //    state: EmailCapture writes both keys, so the next page load fails
      //    this check and the ask never returns.
      try {
        if (localStorage.getItem('SMG_UNLOCKED') === '1' ||
            localStorage.getItem('SMG_EMAIL')) return;
      } catch (e) { /* private mode: show it, it is harmless */ }

      // 3. ITS OWN DISMISSAL KEY, deliberately not the install pitch's. Two
      //    different asks at two different moments: silencing "keep this on
      //    your phone" must not silence "now sync it", and vice versa. Global
      //    across this component's placements, so one X is enough.
      var KEY = 'SMG_POSTINSTALL_OFF';
      try { if (localStorage.getItem(KEY) === '1') return; } catch (e) { /* show */ }

      wrap.hidden = false;

      var close = document.getElementById('piClose');
      if (close) close.addEventListener('click', function () {
        wrap.hidden = true;
        try { localStorage.setItem(KEY, '1'); } catch (e) { /* this session only */ }
      });

      // A SUBMIT IS NOT HIDDEN IMMEDIATELY, ON PURPOSE. EmailCapture answers in
      // place ("check your inbox and tap the confirm link"), and removing the
      // panel would take that message with it. Instead the registration keys it
      // writes are what stop this ever coming back, checked at the top on the
      // next load. Nothing to do here but let it happen.
      //
      // The one thing worth doing is recording the outcome for the funnel,
      // since a registration that came from inside the installed app is a
      // different event from one on a fix article.
      var form = wrap.querySelector('.cap-form');
      if (form) form.addEventListener('submit', function () {
        setTimeout(function () {
          var ok = false;
          try { ok = localStorage.getItem('SMG_UNLOCKED') === '1'; } catch (e) {}
          if (ok && typeof window.gtag === 'function') {
            window.gtag('event', 'post_install_register', { placement: 'post_install' });
          }
        }, 0);
      });
    })();
  </script>
)}

<style is:global>
  /* The panel itself is EmailCapture's. This adds the corner dismiss and the
     outer spacing, nothing else, so the ask looks like every other registration
     panel on the site rather than like a new kind of thing. */
  .pi { position: relative; margin: 2rem 0 0; }
  .pi[hidden] { display: none; }
  /* Same geometry as the install card's X and InstallApp's, for the same
     reason recorded there: a corner never moves, whatever wraps underneath. */
  .pi-close {
    position: absolute; top: 0.3rem; right: 0.3rem;
    width: 34px; height: 34px;
    display: inline-flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;
    background: transparent; border: none; color: var(--text-faint);
    font-size: 20px; line-height: 1; cursor: pointer;
  }
  .pi-close:hover { color: var(--text); }
  /* Room for the X so it never sits on top of the heading. */
  .pi .capture { padding-right: 2.6rem; }
  @media print { .pi { display: none !important; } }
</style>
